From: AmberELEC
Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: RG351MP USB WLAN roaming/stability fixes

Combined fix set for the in-kernel Rockchip vendor rtl8188eu driver on the
RG351MP's external RTL8188EUS USB dongle. Four independent changes that together
stop the mesh-roaming collapse (link stalls until a physical USB re-plug) and let
wpa_supplicant roam proactively between mesh APs:

  1. os_dep/linux/usb_ops_linux.c: don't count unlinked RX urbs (-ECONNRESET/
     -ENOENT) as continual I/O errors, so a single transient transfer fault no
     longer latches bSurpriseRemoved and permanently kills all USB I/O.
  2. os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c + core/rtw_mlme.c + include/rtw_mlme.h:
     implement the cfg80211 set_cqm_rssi_config op plus a watchdog RSSI check, so
     wpa_supplicant's bgscan can enable signal monitoring and roam before the
     link is lost instead of only after a beacon timeout.
  3. include/rtw_mlme.h + os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c: enable the previously
     ifdef'd-out mlmepriv.not_indic_disco guard so a mid-connect disconnect
     indication is not forwarded to cfg80211 (secondary net).
  4. os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c: reject a connect issued while already
     _FW_LINKED|_FW_UNDER_LINKING with -EALREADY (like aircrack-ng rtl8188eus
     v5.3.9), so a roam CONNECT never runs the disruptive select_and_join
     teardown; wpa_supplicant then performs a clean disconnect+connect (primary
     fix).

The four sections MUST stay in this order: their hunk offsets in the shared
files (ioctl_cfg80211.c, rtw_mlme.h) assume the earlier sections are applied
first.


# ===== don't count unlinked (-ECONNRESET/-ENOENT) RX urbs as continual I/O errors =====
#
# From: AmberELEC
# Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: don't count unlinked RX urbs as continual I/O errors
#
# usb_read_port_complete() increments dvobj->continual_io_error on ANY non-zero
# urb->status and latches rtw_set_surprise_removed() once it exceeds
# MAX_CONTINUAL_IO_ERR (4); after that the still-plugged dongle is treated as
# physically removed and all USB I/O stops until a real re-enumeration (physical
# replug) -- WiFi never self-heals.
#
# The driver submits NR_RECVBUFF (8) RX urbs to the bulk-IN endpoint at once. When
# one genuine transfer fault occurs (e.g. -EPROTO during a scan/roam storm), the
# host controller aborts the whole endpoint queue and completes the remaining
# queued urbs with -ECONNRESET (urb unlinked). Each of those driver/HCD-side
# cancellations is then counted as a separate I/O error, so a SINGLE transient
# fault instantly pushes the counter past 4 and permanently bricks the link.
#
# Every mainline USB driver (rtl8150, pegasus, carl9170, rtlwifi, r8152, ...)
# treats -ECONNRESET/-ENOENT as "urb was unlinked / disconnect in progress" and
# returns without counting it. Do the same here: exclude those two statuses from
# the continual_io_error accounting so a transient endpoint fault can no longer
# latch bSurpriseRemoved. Genuine, persistent transfer errors (-EPROTO etc.) are
# still counted and still escalate. Applies to both CONFIG_USE_USB_BUFFER_ALLOC_RX
# variants of usb_read_port_complete().

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/usb_ops_linux.c	2026-09-19 10:50:49.997927032 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/usb_ops_linux.c	2026-09-19 10:50:50.337484934 +0200
@@ -746,7 +746,13 @@
 
 		RTW_INFO("###=> usb_read_port_complete => urb.status(%d)\n", purb->status);
 
-		if (rtw_inc_and_chk_continual_io_error(adapter_to_dvobj(padapter)) == _TRUE)
+		/* -ECONNRESET/-ENOENT mean the urb was unlinked (driver/HCD-side
+		 * cancel; a single transfer fault aborts the whole multi-urb RX
+		 * pipeline). Like every mainline USB driver, do NOT count these as
+		 * hardware I/O errors, otherwise one transient fault trips
+		 * continual_io_error and permanently latches bSurpriseRemoved. */
+		if (purb->status != -ECONNRESET && purb->status != -ENOENT
+			&& rtw_inc_and_chk_continual_io_error(adapter_to_dvobj(padapter)) == _TRUE)
 			rtw_set_surprise_removed(padapter);
 
 		switch (purb->status) {
@@ -907,7 +913,13 @@
 
 		RTW_INFO("###=> usb_read_port_complete => urb.status(%d)\n", purb->status);
 
-		if (rtw_inc_and_chk_continual_io_error(adapter_to_dvobj(padapter)) == _TRUE)
+		/* -ECONNRESET/-ENOENT mean the urb was unlinked (driver/HCD-side
+		 * cancel; a single transfer fault aborts the whole multi-urb RX
+		 * pipeline). Like every mainline USB driver, do NOT count these as
+		 * hardware I/O errors, otherwise one transient fault trips
+		 * continual_io_error and permanently latches bSurpriseRemoved. */
+		if (purb->status != -ECONNRESET && purb->status != -ENOENT
+			&& rtw_inc_and_chk_continual_io_error(adapter_to_dvobj(padapter)) == _TRUE)
 			rtw_set_surprise_removed(padapter);
 
 		switch (purb->status) {

# ===== implement nl80211 CQM RSSI monitoring so wpa_supplicant bgscan can roam proactively =====
#
# From: AmberELEC
# Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: implement nl80211 CQM RSSI monitoring for roaming
#
# The Rockchip vendor rtl8188eu driver never implemented the cfg80211
# set_cqm_rssi_config operation, so wpa_supplicant's "bgscan simple" fails with
# "Failed to enable signal strength monitoring" and can therefore only react
# AFTER the link is already lost (beacon timeout) instead of scanning/roaming
# proactively while the current AP is still weakly reachable. On a device that
# roams between mesh APs this produces multi-second connection drops.
#
# Add a minimal CQM implementation:
#
#   * cfg80211_rtw_set_cqm_rssi_config() stores the RSSI threshold and hysteresis
#     requested by wpa_supplicant and enables monitoring.
#   * rtw_cfg80211_cqm_rssi_check() is called from the existing 2s mlme watchdog
#     (rtw_dynamic_check_timer_handlder); it compares the connected-AP signal
#     (same value get_station reports to userspace) against the threshold and, on
#     a hysteresis-guarded crossing, emits an NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT
#     LOW/HIGH via cfg80211_cqm_rssi_notify().
#
# This lets wpa_supplicant/bgscan trigger a scan when the signal drops below the
# configured level and roam before the link breaks.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/include/rtw_mlme.h	2026-09-19 17:12:50.059408871 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/include/rtw_mlme.h	2026-09-19 17:14:27.051811168 +0200
@@ -588,6 +588,11 @@
 	sint	fw_state;	/* shall we protect this variable? maybe not necessarily... */
 	u8 bScanInProcess;
 	u8	to_join; /* flag */
+	/* nl80211 CQM RSSI monitoring (for wpa_supplicant bgscan roaming) */
+	s32 cqm_rssi_thold;
+	u32 cqm_rssi_hyst;
+	u8 cqm_rssi_enable;
+	int cqm_rssi_last_event;
 #ifdef CONFIG_LAYER2_ROAMING
 	u8 to_roam; /* roaming trying times */
 	struct wlan_network *roam_network; /* the target of active roam */
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 17:12:50.065886971 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 17:14:27.055004769 +0200
@@ -6842,6 +6842,68 @@
 }
 #endif
 
+static int cfg80211_rtw_set_cqm_rssi_config(struct wiphy *wiphy,
+		struct net_device *ndev, s32 rssi_thold, u32 rssi_hyst)
+{
+	_adapter *padapter = (_adapter *)rtw_netdev_priv(ndev);
+	struct mlme_priv *pmlmepriv = &padapter->mlmepriv;
+
+	pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_thold = rssi_thold;
+	pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_hyst = rssi_hyst;
+	pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_last_event = -1;
+	pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_enable = (rssi_thold == 0) ? 0 : 1;
+
+	RTW_INFO(FUNC_NDEV_FMT" thold=%d hyst=%u enable=%u\n", FUNC_NDEV_ARG(ndev),
+		rssi_thold, rssi_hyst, pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_enable);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* Called periodically from the mlme watchdog; emits a cfg80211 CQM RSSI
+ * threshold event when the connected-AP signal crosses the configured
+ * threshold (+/- hysteresis). This is what lets wpa_supplicant's bgscan do
+ * proactive, signal-based roaming instead of only reconnecting after the
+ * link is already lost. */
+void rtw_cfg80211_cqm_rssi_check(_adapter *padapter)
+{
+	struct mlme_priv *pmlmepriv = &padapter->mlmepriv;
+	struct net_device *ndev = padapter->pnetdev;
+	s32 rssi_dbm;
+	int event;
+
+	if (!pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_enable)
+		return;
+	if (!check_fwstate(pmlmepriv, WIFI_STATION_STATE))
+		return;
+	if (!check_fwstate(pmlmepriv, _FW_LINKED))
+		return;
+
+	rssi_dbm = translate_percentage_to_dbm(padapter->recvpriv.signal_strength);
+
+	if (rssi_dbm < pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_thold)
+		event = NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT_LOW;
+	else
+		event = NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT_HIGH;
+
+	if (event == pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_last_event)
+		return;
+
+	/* hysteresis margin around the configured threshold */
+	if (event == NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT_LOW
+	    && rssi_dbm > pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_thold - (s32)pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_hyst)
+		return;
+	if (event == NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT_HIGH
+	    && rssi_dbm < pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_thold + (s32)pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_hyst)
+		return;
+
+	pmlmepriv->cqm_rssi_last_event = event;
+
+	RTW_INFO(FUNC_NDEV_FMT" rssi=%d dBm -> %s\n", FUNC_NDEV_ARG(ndev), rssi_dbm,
+		event == NL80211_CQM_RSSI_THRESHOLD_EVENT_LOW ? "LOW" : "HIGH");
+
+	cfg80211_cqm_rssi_notify(ndev, event, GFP_ATOMIC);
+}
+
 static struct cfg80211_ops rtw_cfg80211_ops = {
 	.change_virtual_intf = cfg80211_rtw_change_iface,
 	.add_key = cfg80211_rtw_add_key,
@@ -6861,6 +6923,7 @@
 	.set_tx_power = cfg80211_rtw_set_txpower,
 	.get_tx_power = cfg80211_rtw_get_txpower,
 	.set_power_mgmt = cfg80211_rtw_set_power_mgmt,
+	.set_cqm_rssi_config = cfg80211_rtw_set_cqm_rssi_config,
 	.set_pmksa = cfg80211_rtw_set_pmksa,
 	.del_pmksa = cfg80211_rtw_del_pmksa,
 	.flush_pmksa = cfg80211_rtw_flush_pmksa,
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/core/rtw_mlme.c	2026-09-19 17:12:50.072267671 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/core/rtw_mlme.c	2026-09-19 17:14:27.058236469 +0200
@@ -3014,6 +3014,10 @@
 	#endif
 }
 
+#ifdef CONFIG_IOCTL_CFG80211
+extern void rtw_cfg80211_cqm_rssi_check(_adapter *adapter);
+#endif
+
 void rtw_dynamic_check_timer_handlder(_adapter *adapter)
 {
 	if (!adapter)
@@ -3031,6 +3035,10 @@
 
 	if (!is_drv_in_lps(adapter))
 		rtw_dynamic_chk_wk_cmd(adapter);
+
+#ifdef CONFIG_IOCTL_CFG80211
+	rtw_cfg80211_cqm_rssi_check(adapter);
+#endif
 }
 
 

# ===== enable not_indic_disco guard: don't forward the mid-connect disconnect (secondary net) =====
#
# From: AmberELEC
# Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: don't forward the mid-connect disconnect to cfg80211
#
# On a wpa_supplicant/CQM-driven roam (connected -> new BSS) the vendor driver
# enters cfg80211_rtw_connect while still _FW_LINKED. rtw_select_and_join_from_
# scanned_queue() then tears down the old association and calls
# rtw_indicate_disconnect(), which reaches rtw_cfg80211_indicate_disconnect() and
# emits cfg80211_disconnected()/connect_result() in the MIDDLE of the connect.
# This zeroes wdev->ssid_len in the cfg80211 core; the subsequent connect_result
# then fails its BSS lookup (WARN_ON(!bss) in __cfg80211_connect_result), returns
# early and never sets wdev->current_bss. The following PTK NEW_KEY is rejected
# ("WPA: Failed to set PTK ... key_flag=0xc"), the 4-Way handshake fails and the
# link stalls until a re-plug.
#
# The driver already carries the fix used by newer rtl8188eus releases: a
# mlmepriv.not_indic_disco flag that is raised for the duration of a cfg80211
# connect and checked in rtw_cfg80211_indicate_disconnect() to suppress the
# spurious event. In this tree the whole mechanism was compiled out behind
# SUPPLICANT_RTK_VERSION_LOWER_THAN_JB42 (undefined). Newer drivers enable it
# unconditionally for kernels >= 4.2 (RTW_CFG80211_BLOCK_DISCON_WHEN_CONNECT);
# do the same here by dropping the ifdef gates around the flag, its set on
# connect entry, its clear on connect exit and the guard in
# rtw_cfg80211_indicate_disconnect(). The user-initiated disconnect path keeps
# its (disabled) block, matching upstream >= 4.2 behaviour, so a real disconnect
# is still reported. The flag is always cleared before cfg80211_rtw_connect
# returns, so a genuine later disconnect always recovers.

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/include/rtw_mlme.h	2026-09-19 17:52:45.308505718 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/include/rtw_mlme.h	2026-09-19 17:53:05.642778885 +0200
@@ -607,9 +607,10 @@
 
 	u8	*nic_hdl;
 
-#ifdef SUPPLICANT_RTK_VERSION_LOWER_THAN_JB42
+	/* set while a cfg80211 connect is in progress so a spurious
+	 * mid-connect disconnect indication is NOT forwarded to cfg80211
+	 * (mirrors newer rtl8188eus RTW_CFG80211_BLOCK_DISCON_WHEN_CONNECT) */
 	u8	not_indic_disco;
-#endif
 	_list		*pscanned;
 	_queue	free_bss_pool;
 	_queue	scanned_queue;
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 17:52:45.310810218 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 17:53:05.650191585 +0200
@@ -816,11 +816,7 @@
 	}
 #endif /* CONFIG_P2P */
 
-	#ifdef SUPPLICANT_RTK_VERSION_LOWER_THAN_JB42
 	if (!padapter->mlmepriv.not_indic_disco || padapter->ndev_unregistering) {
-	#else
-	{
-	#endif
 		#if LINUX_VERSION_CODE < KERNEL_VERSION(3, 11, 0) || defined(COMPAT_KERNEL_RELEASE)
 		RTW_INFO("pwdev->sme_state(b)=%d\n", pwdev->sme_state);
 
@@ -3055,9 +3051,7 @@
 	struct security_priv *psecuritypriv = &padapter->securitypriv;
 	_queue *queue = &pmlmepriv->scanned_queue;
 
-#ifdef SUPPLICANT_RTK_VERSION_LOWER_THAN_JB42
 	padapter->mlmepriv.not_indic_disco = _TRUE;
-#endif
 
 	RTW_INFO("=>"FUNC_NDEV_FMT" - Start to Connection\n", FUNC_NDEV_ARG(ndev));
 	RTW_INFO("privacy=%d, key=%p, key_len=%d, key_idx=%d, auth_type=%d\n",
@@ -3272,9 +3266,7 @@
 exit:
 	RTW_INFO("<=%s, ret %d\n", __FUNCTION__, ret);
 
-#ifdef SUPPLICANT_RTK_VERSION_LOWER_THAN_JB42
 	padapter->mlmepriv.not_indic_disco = _FALSE;
-#endif
 
 	return ret;
 }

# ===== reject connect while already linked/linking with -EALREADY (primary fix) =====
#
# From: AmberELEC
# Subject: [PATCH] rtl8188eu: reject connect while already linked/linking (-EALREADY)
#
# A wpa_supplicant/connman driven roam issues NL80211_CMD_CONNECT to the new
# BSS while the driver is still _FW_LINKED to the old AP. The old vendor
# driver runs the full rtw_set_802_11_connect() -> rtw_do_join()/to_join ->
# rtw_select_and_join_from_scanned_queue() path, whose _FW_LINKED teardown
# calls rtw_indicate_disconnect() -> cfg80211_disconnected() in the middle of
# the connect. That zeroes wdev->ssid_len, so the subsequent connect_result
# hits WARN_ON(!bss) in __cfg80211_connect_result(), leaves current_bss NULL,
# the nl80211 core then rejects the PTK NEW_KEY ("WPA: Failed to set PTK"),
# the 4-Way handshake fails and the link stalls until a USB re-plug.
#
# Newer rtl8188eus (aircrack-ng v5.3.9) reject such a redundant connect early
# with -EALREADY when _FW_LINKED | _FW_UNDER_LINKING, so the disruptive
# teardown never runs; wpa_supplicant then does a clean disconnect+connect.
# Port that early return. (patch 010's not_indic_disco is only the secondary
# net and does not cover this teardown, which runs from the cmd thread's
# surveydone callback after the connect flag was already cleared.)

--- a/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 18:16:12.410543333 +0200
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/rockchip_wlan/rtl8188eu/os_dep/linux/ioctl_cfg80211.c	2026-09-19 18:16:12.427807133 +0200
@@ -3064,6 +3064,15 @@
 		goto exit;
 	}
 
+	/* don't tear down an existing/pending association from a redundant
+	 * (e.g. roam) connect request; reject early like newer rtl8188eus so
+	 * the mid-connect rtw_indicate_disconnect never corrupts cfg80211 SME */
+	if (check_fwstate(pmlmepriv, _FW_LINKED | _FW_UNDER_LINKING) == _TRUE) {
+		ret = -EALREADY;
+		RTW_INFO("%s skip connect! fw_state=0x%x\n", __func__, pmlmepriv->fw_state);
+		goto exit;
+	}
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_PLATFORM_MSTAR_SCAN_BEFORE_CONNECT
 	printk("MStar Android!\n");
 	if (adapter_wdev_data(padapter)->bandroid_scan == _FALSE) {
